          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus identifies the palm primarily with the righteous person’s flourishing, uprightness, vitality, and fruitfulness, while also recognizing victory and triumph as a major palm-branch association. Temple palms are generally treated as sacred-space, life, or Eden imagery rather than unambiguously as representations of righteous people.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book identifies the palm directly as “the righteous” and gathers Psalm 92, temple carvings, festal or royal branches, and the palms held by Revelation’s white-robed multitude under that identification. It also notes the lexical connection between kappot and the open palm of a hand.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Palm tree = the flourishing and fruitful righteous person, with palm branches also commonly signifying victory or triumph.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Palm = the righteous.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book appears to extend the righteous-person symbolism beyond Psalm 92 to temple palm carvings and palms held by the redeemed. The consensus recognizes those passages but commonly classifies the temple imagery as sacred life/Eden and the handheld branches as rejoicing or victory rather than treating every instance as a direct emblem of the righteous.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction at the core: both identify the palm with the righteous. The difference concerns whether that identification governs the additional temple and branch contexts.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the consensus’s clearest central identification but applies it more uniformly across contexts that the consensus treats as carrying related yet distinct meanings. This is a material broadening of extension, so REFINED is more precise than MATCH; it does not replace the consensus referent and therefore is not DIVERGENT.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Psalm 92 directly supports the righteous-palm identification, but 1 Kings 6, Ezekiel 41, Leviticus 23, John 12, and Revelation 7 merely show palms in temple, festival, royal-acclamation, or heavenly settings. They do not independently state that those palms symbolize the righteous, so the book’s cross-context unification is inferential.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
